  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 4.2. Strengthened capacity of enterprises to adopt new business models, technology and techniques to enhance productivity and sustainability</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Between January to August 2020, the Government of Sierra Leone and other national stakeholders including the Sierra Leone Labour Congress promoted innovative business development models with a particular focus on women in rural areas. As a result, eight farming groups in Bombali, Port Loko, Bo and Kenema formed cooperatives and were registered by the Department of Cooperatives under the Ministry of Trade and Industry between June to August 2020. Overall, the trainings provided to improve and adapt (agricultural) business practices, promote innovative and gender-sensitive financial products, support the transition of existing farming groups into cooperatives, and address negative perceptions about women in business. The training-of-trainers approach ensures that capacities built remain within the targeted communities
The National Youth Commission (NAYCOM) conducted a Women&#x2019;s Entrepreneurship Self-Check (WE Check) to promote the relevance and sustainability of entrepreneurship programmes with a focus on women entrepreneurs. The report of the assessment was finalized and lunched in June 2021. The findings of the report include lack of data on the &#x201C;nexus between entrepreneurship and gender&#x201D;. During a survey conducted among NAYCOM clients, some 80 per cent of women indicated access to finance as a challenge in starting a business. The report recommends the development of a gender policy for NAYCOM. 
Technical trainings were complemented by sensitization workshops for both men and women to raise awareness on the role of women in businesses. The ILO supported the capacity of national stakeholders including the Sierra Leone Labour Congress (SLLC), civil society organizations and identified &#x201C;gender champions&#x201D; through a training-of-trainers approach in the areas of gender and entrepreneurship (July 2019), business development (October 2019), financial education (October 2019), cooperative development (November 2019) and occupational safety and health (November 2019). 
The ILO updated and expanded a previous assessment of the supply of and demand for financial services in Sierra Leone in 2020. The WE Check, which was conducted by NAYCOM with support from the ILO,  acknowledged women&#x2019;s exclusion from financial services and confirmed their potential for women&#x2019;s socio-economic empowerment. In July 2020, the ILO supported capacities of financial institutions to diversify their product portfolio through the ILO&#x2019;s Making Microfinance Work training programme.</narrative>
   </description>
